00:36:32,472 --> 00:36:38,294
[SPEAKER_04]: It's more like, what is the presently enduring feeling in regard to this presently enduring intention?

341
00:36:39,114 --> 00:36:40,975
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's where your craving will be rooted in.

342
00:36:42,860 --> 00:36:47,582
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why often you'll be acting out of those intentions, revolving around them, having more thoughts.

343
00:36:48,042 --> 00:36:50,603
[SPEAKER_04]: It's because they happen to be pressuring you.

344
00:36:50,763 --> 00:36:54,224
[SPEAKER_04]: And they're pressuring you because you were unaware of the anicca of those phenomena.

345
00:36:56,445 --> 00:36:57,346
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always pain.

346
00:36:57,386 --> 00:37:01,227
[SPEAKER_02]: It's always... there's always this... Yeah.

347
00:37:01,267 --> 00:37:01,547
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

348
00:37:01,587 --> 00:37:04,008
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether it's craving for pleasure or...

349
00:37:04,028 --> 00:37:04,529
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

350
00:37:04,709 --> 00:37:05,289
[SPEAKER_04]: Anger.

351
00:37:05,369 --> 00:37:07,570
[SPEAKER_04]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

352
00:37:07,810 --> 00:37:10,571
[SPEAKER_04]: To the same extent craving is there, to that extent suffering is there.

353
00:37:11,675 --> 00:37:14,477
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not like sometimes craving disappears but suffering stays.

354
00:37:14,497 --> 00:37:14,637
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

355
00:37:14,657 --> 00:37:20,120
[SPEAKER_04]: Literally, presence of craving is what is felt as suffering from the point of view of feeling.

356
00:37:20,781 --> 00:37:21,281
[SPEAKER_04]: Nothing else.

357
00:37:22,742 --> 00:37:28,786
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you remove the craving as presence, there is nothing to be felt.

358
00:37:30,567 --> 00:37:35,050
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not like craving then produces this process that results in suffering.

359
00:37:35,110 --> 00:37:35,290
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

360
00:37:36,762 --> 00:37:41,447
[SPEAKER_04]: The Four Noble Truths is the Presence of Craving, Presence of Suffering, Absence of Craving, Absence of Suffering.

361
00:37:42,267 --> 00:37:46,531
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you want to then learn how to think without a craving, act without a craving, live without a craving.

362
00:37:46,972 --> 00:37:50,275
[SPEAKER_04]: You will then learn how to be free from suffering, perpetually.

363
00:37:52,237 --> 00:37:54,099
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what the Noble Eightfold Path would be.

364
00:37:58,783 --> 00:38:04,646
[SPEAKER_04]: So yeah, that disease, that uneasiness that you discern, on account of anything that's presently enduring, yeah.

365
00:38:05,126 --> 00:38:08,187
[SPEAKER_04]: So now, you want to see, what is my attitude?

366
00:38:08,227 --> 00:38:09,148
[SPEAKER_04]: What is my intention?

367
00:38:09,528 --> 00:38:13,210
[SPEAKER_04]: What is my current feeling in regard to this uneasiness that I'm discerning?

368
00:38:13,730 --> 00:38:14,771
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I trying to get rid of it?

369
00:38:15,951 --> 00:38:18,252
[SPEAKER_04]: Because uneasiness in itself is not suffering.

370
00:38:18,913 --> 00:38:20,513
[SPEAKER_04]: Feeling in itself is not suffering.

371
00:38:22,654 --> 00:38:25,956
[SPEAKER_04]: Every feeling counts as suffering, while you're not free from craving.

372
00:38:26,867 --> 00:38:29,729
[SPEAKER_04]: But in itself, aggregative feeling is not what dukkha is.

373
00:38:31,030 --> 00:38:37,355
[SPEAKER_04]: So, even that uneasiness, it's only suffering, because implicitly, you want to get rid of it.

374
00:38:38,256 --> 00:38:45,622
[SPEAKER_04]: And you're not seeing that fundamental, subtle, yet kind of obvious attitude, that you know about, but you're not seeing it.

375
00:38:46,082 --> 00:38:49,164
[SPEAKER_04]: In this instance, on this level, it's not apparent to you.

376
00:38:49,785 --> 00:38:51,026
[SPEAKER_04]: Hence, suffering is there.

377
00:39:12,223 --> 00:39:25,915
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's always ignorance, there's always some ignoring taking place actively right now, like, unless you're an arhat.

378
00:39:27,817 --> 00:39:32,962
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yeah, I think it's like what I said in one of those talks, you know,

379
00:39:34,381 --> 00:39:37,965
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to learn, like, it's part of the existence.

380
00:39:38,345 --> 00:39:40,007
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't think about everything all the time.

381
00:39:40,568 --> 00:39:41,148
[SPEAKER_04]: It's impossible.

382
00:39:41,168 --> 00:39:42,209
[SPEAKER_04]: Even the Buddha couldn't do that.

383
00:39:44,452 --> 00:39:50,378
[SPEAKER_04]: You think about certain things, which means other things must be sort of forgotten at a time.

384
00:39:52,700 --> 00:39:56,865
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's when you are not free from craving, that forgetting means ignoring.

385
00:39:58,076 --> 00:40:03,818
[SPEAKER_04]: When you're free from craving, that forgetting is literally just forgetting, you know, thinking about those things specifically.

386
00:40:04,258 --> 00:40:12,181
[SPEAKER_04]: But when you do remember, you don't... it doesn't come back with that ignoring, with that layer of... of not... lack of understanding with it.

387
00:40:12,381 --> 00:40:14,861
[SPEAKER_04]: So, whatever arises is fully, clearly understood.

388
00:40:15,662 --> 00:40:19,443
[SPEAKER_04]: When it arises, through its own arising, through its own enduring and manifestation.

389
00:40:21,428 --> 00:40:26,153
[SPEAKER_04]: So, ignoring is not... it's not this mystical... it's not this mystical thing that you must...

390
00:40:26,173 --> 00:40:29,137
[SPEAKER_04]: I must find out things that ignore... that I ignore.

391
00:40:29,617 --> 00:40:39,529
[SPEAKER_04]: Ignoring is that attitude that you have right here and now, for example, not being thoroughly clear about the nature of present enduring feeling that you have on that... on that right level.

392
00:40:39,989 --> 00:40:45,254
[SPEAKER_04]: Not of a specific feeling, but presently and during feeling the mood, you are not clear about it.

393
00:40:45,294 --> 00:40:53,020
[SPEAKER_04]: Which means, to the extent you're not clear about it, to that extent, there will be some gratuitous resistance to it, or leaning towards it.

394
00:40:53,681 --> 00:40:55,002
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, craving will be there.

395
00:41:01,863 --> 00:41:13,071
[SPEAKER_04]: And that clarity I'm talking about, that you must develop, it's not on the level of figuring it out as an idea, but it's clarity right here, right now, on the level of your attitude towards your own feeling.

396
00:41:13,752 --> 00:41:14,612
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you resisting it?

397
00:41:14,772 --> 00:41:15,573
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you ignoring it?

398
00:41:15,673 --> 00:41:21,478
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you indulging it when it's pleasant?

399
00:41:23,079 --> 00:41:27,062
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's something you need to learn how to sustain throughout the day.

400
00:41:30,821 --> 00:41:36,325
[SPEAKER_04]: That peripheral recognition of present enduring feeling, of this kind, of that kind, doesn't matter.

401
00:41:36,665 --> 00:41:37,786
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not trying to get rid of it.

402
00:41:37,826 --> 00:41:38,886
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not trying to prolong it.

403
00:41:39,126 --> 00:41:41,508
[SPEAKER_04]: And you're certainly not trying to forget about it or ignore it.

404
00:41:41,788 --> 00:41:43,189
[SPEAKER_04]: You can forget other things if you want.

405
00:41:43,970 --> 00:41:46,491
[SPEAKER_04]: But not the things... not the point where things converge.

406
00:41:47,732 --> 00:41:49,553
[SPEAKER_04]: Not the mindfulness of your body, for example.

407
00:41:49,933 --> 00:41:54,977
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, that's why these things are reference points or anchors in satipaṭṭhāna, whichever you want to translate it.

408
00:41:57,385 --> 00:42:09,389
[SPEAKER_02]: When you say where they converge, is it meaning any sort of proliferated manifestation?

409
00:42:09,789 --> 00:42:11,190
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it means where things meet.

410
00:42:12,289 --> 00:42:19,118
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you have this idea, for example, this intention, that it's pressuring you to do something, say it's an unwholesome intention.

411
00:42:19,819 --> 00:42:25,948
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, the craving is not in the intention, nor in your action, so to speak, doing, acting out of intention.

412
00:42:26,448 --> 00:42:29,633
[SPEAKER_04]: The craving is that simultaneously present feeling with that intention.

413
00:42:31,267 --> 00:42:33,048
[SPEAKER_04]: So, things meet there.

414
00:42:33,549 --> 00:42:40,294
[SPEAKER_04]: So, on one hand, you have one intention, but then you have another felt feeling, and your craving is basically on the level of the feeling, not on the level of intention.

415
00:42:40,655 --> 00:42:41,836
[SPEAKER_04]: But they meet in that feeling.

416
00:42:42,396 --> 00:42:52,604
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you are acting very clearly, very consciously, and that's why craving appears to be part of, like, in this intention, in this thought, in this desire, in this product of my action.

417
00:42:53,425 --> 00:42:53,885
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's not.

418
00:42:53,925 --> 00:42:57,148
[SPEAKER_04]: It's always in the... in regard to how you feel, not what you intend to.

419
00:43:00,893 --> 00:43:03,855
[SPEAKER_04]: But those things are inseparable.

420
00:43:03,915 --> 00:43:04,835
[SPEAKER_04]: Feeling and intention.

421
00:43:05,475 --> 00:43:17,281
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you stop maintaining fe... er... craving in regard to your feeling, then you can intend, you can feel, you can perceive, and none of that will be bundled and together with that craving.

422
00:43:18,242 --> 00:43:21,303
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the feeling will cease to serve as a convergence point.

423
00:43:22,724 --> 00:43:25,946
[SPEAKER_04]: Only for as long as there is feeling, there will be converging on the...

424
00:43:26,266 --> 00:43:26,526
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry.

425
00:43:26,666 --> 00:43:30,008
[SPEAKER_04]: Only as long as there is craving, there will be converging on the feeling.

426
00:43:31,052 --> 00:43:31,972
[SPEAKER_04]: Things without craving.

427
00:43:38,755 --> 00:43:42,436
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, in other words, that converging is that pressure, that contact.

428
00:43:43,577 --> 00:43:43,877
[SPEAKER_02]: Passive.

429
00:43:43,897 --> 00:43:44,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Like sense of self.

430
00:43:46,197 --> 00:43:47,598
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's all what comes out of it.

431
00:43:49,995 --> 00:43:52,977
[SPEAKER_04]: It's that, basically, direction that craving gives you.

432
00:43:53,017 --> 00:44:02,481
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, for example, as I said, you discern the phenomenon of this universal uneasiness, but you haven't discerned the direction of your resistance away from it.

433
00:44:02,641 --> 00:44:04,262
[SPEAKER_04]: The direction of, I don't want this.

434
00:44:06,123 --> 00:44:10,725
[SPEAKER_04]: Yet, it's there, with it, met with it, gratuitously, but it's there, and that's why you suffer.

435
00:44:11,326 --> 00:44:15,828
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because things are uneasy, but because you keep trying to, sort of, squeeze yourself out of it.

436
00:44:17,549 --> 00:44:18,710
[SPEAKER_04]: You want it to go away.

437
00:44:18,750 --> 00:44:19,831
[SPEAKER_04]: You want to get rid of it.

438
00:44:21,512 --> 00:44:22,973
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why it's unpleasant.

439
00:44:23,053 --> 00:44:24,514
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is why you want to get rid of it.

440
00:44:24,774 --> 00:44:25,895
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is why it's unpleasant.

441
00:44:25,915 --> 00:44:26,995
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is why you want to get rid of it.

442
00:44:27,556 --> 00:44:28,797
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's another vicious circle.

443
00:44:32,899 --> 00:44:37,663
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this is essentially the sutra on the two darts, isn't it?

444
00:44:37,703 --> 00:44:45,728
[SPEAKER_03]: Where it says that a patujana does not know how to deal with unpleasant feeling, other than putting sensual pleasure over against it.

445
00:44:46,605 --> 00:44:48,505
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, aggravating the whole situation.

446
00:44:49,186 --> 00:44:49,386
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

447
00:44:49,946 --> 00:44:50,106
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

448
00:44:50,126 --> 00:44:57,047
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when he's struck with the first dart of uneasiness, he's struck with the second dart of not knowing the escape from that uneasiness.

449
00:44:57,308 --> 00:44:58,808
[SPEAKER_04]: As in, he will be resisting it.

450
00:44:59,208 --> 00:45:00,408
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the true suffering.

451
00:45:00,948 --> 00:45:03,709
[SPEAKER_04]: The noble disciple doesn't get the second dart.

452
00:45:06,010 --> 00:45:07,990
[SPEAKER_04]: So, all he has is uneasiness, but he's not resisting it.

453
00:45:09,470 --> 00:45:16,312
[SPEAKER_04]: And because Bhattacharya doesn't know the escape from it, the only escape he knows is to then try to basically cover it up with pleasure of any other kind.

454
00:45:17,553 --> 00:45:22,294
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's where sensuality is sort of intoxicating on two levels.

455
00:45:22,554 --> 00:45:38,058
[SPEAKER_04]: Not just because it provides you with pleasure, but it simultaneously masks the uneasiness, covers up the pain, which is the only way of you, at the time, the only way you know how to deal with the pain.

456
00:45:39,058 --> 00:45:41,799
[SPEAKER_04]: That's where sensuality needs to be restrained first, out of faith.

457
00:45:43,703 --> 00:45:56,343
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, if you don't give in to sensuality as a way of masking the fact that things are universally uneasy, then your attitude of... gratuitous attitude of resisting the uneasiness will become apparent.

458
00:45:56,423 --> 00:45:58,085
[SPEAKER_04]: On the level it is.

459
00:45:58,105 --> 00:45:58,867
[SPEAKER_04]: Not in theory.

460
00:46:00,253 --> 00:46:01,775
[SPEAKER_04]: And then when you... that's when you don't suffer.

461
00:46:01,815 --> 00:46:04,157
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you... basically, sensuality becomes redundant.

462
00:46:04,858 --> 00:46:07,861
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't need it anymore, because now you don't suffer to begin with.

463
00:46:08,261 --> 00:46:13,747
[SPEAKER_04]: You are not hit with that, like, dark... the tone... the tone of pain that's been pulled out.

464
00:46:16,870 --> 00:46:22,475
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's the recognition of that, that uneasy feeling, pleasant feeling, unpleasant feeling, is not where the craving is.

465
00:46:23,305 --> 00:46:25,567
[SPEAKER_04]: It's in your attitude towards present enduring feeling.

466
00:46:25,607 --> 00:46:27,769
[SPEAKER_04]: Not, oh, I felt this, thus I crave.

467
00:46:27,789 --> 00:46:27,969
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

468
00:46:28,249 --> 00:46:34,734
[SPEAKER_04]: Currently, now, while I'm thinking or trying to figure these things out, what is the present enduring feeling?

469
00:46:34,914 --> 00:46:43,881
[SPEAKER_04]: That's where the craving, that leaning towards direction, or leaning away direction, or basically turning a blind eye direction, will be maintained and cultivated.

470
00:46:46,443 --> 00:46:49,846
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why, like, developing mindfulness that teaches you, basically, how to

471
00:46:51,000 --> 00:47:00,868
[SPEAKER_04]: Stop being absorbed in immediate objects of your senses, so that you can see what current feeling... because feeling is not in your senses, nor in your sense organs, nor in the sense objects.

472
00:47:02,070 --> 00:47:05,512
[SPEAKER_04]: It's pretty much as that background to it, as a context to those things.

473
00:47:10,016 --> 00:47:16,502
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you want to learn how to maintain the site of that context on that peripheral level.

474
00:47:21,008 --> 00:47:23,209
[SPEAKER_04]: That's when those interrogations were did on YouTube.

475
00:47:23,829 --> 00:47:26,490
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, oh, how do I feel?

476
00:47:26,590 --> 00:47:28,211
[SPEAKER_04]: Not theoretically, not abstractly.

477
00:47:28,271 --> 00:47:30,472
[SPEAKER_04]: While I'm asking the question, what is my current feeling?

478
00:47:30,552 --> 00:47:32,893
[SPEAKER_04]: What is my current overall feeling, enduring?

479
00:47:33,734 --> 00:47:36,515
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, not okay, a bit of both.

480
00:47:36,535 --> 00:47:37,075
[SPEAKER_04]: Which one is it?

481
00:47:37,155 --> 00:47:42,257
[SPEAKER_04]: And then learned, it's ambiguous to begin with, but the more you look in that direction, the clearer it will become.

482
00:47:42,297 --> 00:47:45,039
[SPEAKER_04]: Not, you will not make it an object of your attention.

483
00:47:45,059 --> 00:47:48,040
[SPEAKER_04]: It's gonna stay a feeling, but now, you cannot ignore it.

484
00:47:49,306 --> 00:47:50,387
[SPEAKER_04]: Uneasiness endures.

485
00:47:50,907 --> 00:47:51,728
[SPEAKER_04]: Easiness endures.

486
00:47:52,768 --> 00:47:53,889
[SPEAKER_04]: Neither endures.

487
00:47:55,290 --> 00:48:05,697
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's already the first step towards, kind of, uprooting that gratuitous leaning towards it when it's pleasant, and leaning away when it's unpleasant, that you are not even aware of, yet you're doing it.

488
00:48:08,379 --> 00:48:15,424
[SPEAKER_04]: But the only reason you're not aware of it, is because that whole dimension, that peripheral sort of context,

489
00:48:16,580 --> 00:48:17,300
[SPEAKER_04]: Hasn't been developed.

490
00:48:17,661 --> 00:48:19,281
[SPEAKER_04]: The mind hasn't been developed enough.

491
00:48:24,724 --> 00:48:27,506
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this is then where the sense restraint comes in.

492
00:48:27,826 --> 00:48:31,088
[SPEAKER_03]: If there's no escape from unpleasant feeling,

493
00:48:31,490 --> 00:48:34,832
[SPEAKER_03]: Other than sensual pleasure for the ordinary person.

494
00:48:34,972 --> 00:48:36,153
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, sensory strain, yes.

495
00:48:36,253 --> 00:48:46,620
[SPEAKER_04]: But also, see, many people would be sensory strained, but then they would misunderstand the dhamma and meditation, and then their practice would be about focusing on various objects of their attention.

496
00:48:47,220 --> 00:48:50,042
[SPEAKER_04]: Usually, it comes down to focusing on the objects of your senses.

497
00:48:50,983 --> 00:48:56,486
[SPEAKER_04]: Whether it's the object of touch, my body, whether it's sights, sounds, whatever else.

498
00:48:57,086 --> 00:49:00,729
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's already the direction you will not discern craving in.

499
00:49:02,264 --> 00:49:09,808
[SPEAKER_04]: That, where you are focusing, it's already the product of you either leaning towards pleasure, or leaning away from pain, or ignoring the neutral feeling.

500
00:49:11,049 --> 00:49:15,652
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't make that feeling an object of your attention in that sense.

501
00:49:15,672 --> 00:49:16,612
[SPEAKER_04]: It's always peripheral.

502
00:49:20,014 --> 00:49:22,396
[SPEAKER_04]: You will not find the feeling in your body either.

503
00:49:23,716 --> 00:49:25,337
[SPEAKER_04]: It's always fundamentally feeling.

504
00:49:25,417 --> 00:49:28,039
[SPEAKER_04]: Every feeling, as the Sutta says, cetas, it's mental.

505
00:49:30,476 --> 00:49:51,934
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, would you say that when this, when feeling, when vedanā is discerned peripherally, by not running away from it through sensuality, then to see that, then to see that feeling arises independence upon a sense base, and then to abandon, then to abandon sense base as

506
00:49:52,754 --> 00:49:54,035
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, not mine.

507
00:49:54,715 --> 00:50:00,339
[SPEAKER_03]: It has been born as we talked earlier, but I had no say in that sense-base being there.

508
00:50:01,159 --> 00:50:07,363
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, am I not pulling the work away from underneath the feeling and the appropriation that goes with it?

509
00:50:08,904 --> 00:50:14,367
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, you wouldn't... you can't just, like, abandon the sense-base in that sense.

510
00:50:15,701 --> 00:50:33,314
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you're aware of the present enduring feeling that makes you either lean towards the sense base, or try to get rid of the things experienced through your sense base, or ignore it, being aware of that feeling already, as I said, already impedes that gratuitous attitude of craving towards, against, or turning away from it.

511
00:50:34,235 --> 00:50:39,879
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you sustain that, you will cease to act out of senses, you will cease to be bound by them, eventually.

512
00:50:42,883 --> 00:50:49,627
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the product of your appropriation of the senses is a symptom of your craving in regard to the feeling.

513
00:50:49,747 --> 00:50:50,867
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not the problem in itself.

514
00:50:52,128 --> 00:50:56,350
[SPEAKER_04]: You do it only because you're already leaning away or towards your feelings.

515
00:50:57,351 --> 00:50:59,372
[SPEAKER_04]: But, as I said, they are invisible in front of you.

516
00:50:59,392 --> 00:51:00,912
[SPEAKER_04]: All you see is sense and sense objects.

517
00:51:01,032 --> 00:51:02,193
[SPEAKER_04]: Like sense objects, really.

518
00:51:02,273 --> 00:51:03,494
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't even see your sense organs.

519
00:51:04,388 --> 00:51:08,771
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why immediate assumption that, oh, this is what I want and this is what I don't want.

520
00:51:08,791 --> 00:51:17,018
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why when people suffer, eh... ignorantly, the first thing they do is they blame the thing that they perceived that made them suffer.

521
00:51:18,018 --> 00:51:22,702
[SPEAKER_04]: Because they don't see that it's their attitude in regard to how they felt when they perceived that.

522
00:51:23,062 --> 00:51:24,583
[SPEAKER_04]: That's where the suffering is rooted.

523
00:51:25,864 --> 00:51:29,467
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you say something that upsets me, I blame you for my suffering.

524
00:51:30,781 --> 00:51:38,303
[SPEAKER_04]: And for as long as I don't look, basically, peripherally from you, I will never see the root problem of my suffering.

525
00:51:39,404 --> 00:51:48,466
[SPEAKER_04]: And same, like, when there is a prospect of pleasure, I don't see it peripherally as my attitude towards that agreeable prospect of my feeling.

526
00:51:49,167 --> 00:51:51,927
[SPEAKER_04]: I see the sense organ... sense object, sorry.

527
00:51:52,628 --> 00:51:53,828
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I want that then.

528
00:51:55,105 --> 00:52:01,953
[SPEAKER_04]: And for as long as I don't learn how to restrain and look beyond it, while it's still there, I will not see where the problem is really rooted.

529
00:52:04,196 --> 00:52:10,964
[SPEAKER_04]: So that way, it's not like, oh, I restrain, then I see my feeling, then I say no to sense organs, then I have uprooted it.

530
00:52:13,420 --> 00:52:13,600
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no.

531
00:52:13,921 --> 00:52:15,762
[SPEAKER_03]: I wasn't talking in such a...

532
00:52:15,782 --> 00:52:16,503
[SPEAKER_03]: I know, I know.

533
00:52:16,523 --> 00:52:21,147
[SPEAKER_04]: But it can sound like that, if you set it up as this list.

534
00:52:21,187 --> 00:52:27,753
[SPEAKER_04]: But the point is, if you see the context of your feeling correctly and during, you're already withdrawn from the pull of the sense organs.

535
00:52:28,253 --> 00:52:31,276
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if there is still a pressure there, the fact is you're seeing more now.

536
00:52:31,836 --> 00:52:33,198
[SPEAKER_04]: You are not rooted in that pressure.

537
00:52:33,658 --> 00:52:35,379
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you sustain that long enough,

538
00:52:36,160 --> 00:52:47,578
[SPEAKER_04]: Either through contemplating impermanence of those sense organs upon which your own feeling depends, or the impermanence of the gratuitous there is a feeling that you're leaning towards or against, you will be removing the appropriation from it.

539
00:52:49,628 --> 00:52:58,815
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, by seeing that there cannot be any feeling without there being sense bases... Well, no, by seeing that... No, see, you have no experience of no feeling.

540
00:52:59,295 --> 00:53:01,397
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what you just said would be a pure abstraction.

541
00:53:01,997 --> 00:53:03,198
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, there can be no feeling.

542
00:53:03,678 --> 00:53:06,700
[SPEAKER_04]: That's inconceivable for you, how that would be without a feeling.

543
00:53:08,122 --> 00:53:12,645
[SPEAKER_04]: Instead, you want to see that whatever feeling is there, how has it arisen?

544
00:53:13,982 --> 00:53:23,272
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you even conceive how the feeling comes to be, or you just find yourself under it, so to speak, or affected by it, or inside of you?

545
00:53:23,292 --> 00:53:32,081
[SPEAKER_04]: Whichever way, you find the feeling there, and you are not privy to its origins, where it came, and how it came, and why it came, and where it will go.

546
00:53:32,842 --> 00:53:36,226
[SPEAKER_04]: All you're privy to is the pressure that it exerts over you.

547
00:53:37,968 --> 00:53:49,431
[SPEAKER_04]: And you perpetuate that pressure by choosing to then engage with getting more of that thing that made you feel pleasant, or trying to get rid and get less of that thing that made you feel unpleasant.

548
00:53:51,292 --> 00:53:57,113
[SPEAKER_04]: So, whatever's feelings there, you want to understand its own anicca thoroughly, and you will stop leaning towards or leaning away.

549
00:53:57,133 --> 00:54:00,514
[SPEAKER_04]: You will stop trying to get rid of it or appropriate it even more.

550
00:54:00,754 --> 00:54:04,495
[SPEAKER_04]: And you will suddenly be unable to ignore it, ignore the nature of it.

551
00:54:05,839 --> 00:54:21,765
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't mean you need to constantly think about it, but it means, oh, when you basically recollect yourself upon the present enduring feeling, the true... the nature that became apparent of that feeling is seen with that recollection.

552
00:54:22,666 --> 00:54:25,927
[SPEAKER_04]: That's when things become apparent and you understood the nature of it.

553
00:54:28,370 --> 00:54:29,830
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's when wisdom starts taking place.

554
00:54:29,851 --> 00:54:30,731
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why it's effortless.

555
00:54:31,251 --> 00:54:33,932
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not that every time you feel, you have to go through these motions.

556
00:54:33,952 --> 00:54:39,514
[SPEAKER_04]: You do that as a part of training, but if the understanding is taking place, you will have to do it less and less.

557
00:54:41,114 --> 00:54:44,555
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, eventually, when you recollect the feeling, you recollect the true nature of it.

558
00:54:44,575 --> 00:54:52,218
[SPEAKER_04]: It means there is no room for any craving to arise, because the basis for craving is forgetting, ignoring the true nature of it, lapsing from it.

559
00:55:03,538 --> 00:55:11,742
[SPEAKER_04]: So, even when you're not thinking about a particular feeling that's now specifically enduring there in your experience, that feeling is still enduring there in your experience.

560
00:55:11,782 --> 00:55:12,903
[SPEAKER_04]: That's where you can recollect it.

561
00:55:13,323 --> 00:55:14,103
[SPEAKER_04]: You can go back to it.

562
00:55:16,444 --> 00:55:26,209
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when you go back to it, you want to come back with as much wisdom that you... or knowledge, understanding of it, that you developed beforehand, you want to come back with it, to it, and see it clearly.

563
00:55:26,489 --> 00:55:27,209
[SPEAKER_04]: That should be your goal.

564
00:55:28,230 --> 00:55:30,271
[SPEAKER_04]: Up until then, whenever you think it, you think it rightly.

565
00:55:30,991 --> 00:55:32,572
[SPEAKER_04]: No room for distortion anymore.

566
00:55:43,526 --> 00:55:45,668
[SPEAKER_02]: That it's arisen by itself.

567
00:55:46,768 --> 00:55:47,169
[SPEAKER_04]: It's there.

568
00:55:49,450 --> 00:55:55,674
[SPEAKER_04]: Being clear about the nature of a thing being there, to the extent necessary, it's basically the fulfillment of satipaṭṭhāna.

569
00:55:57,135 --> 00:55:59,657
[SPEAKER_04]: He knows the body is there, to the extent necessary.

570
00:56:00,057 --> 00:56:02,779
[SPEAKER_04]: He knows the feeling is just there, to the extent necessary.

571
00:56:04,122 --> 00:56:06,964
[SPEAKER_04]: Extend necessary for the final knowledge.

572
00:56:07,024 --> 00:56:07,365
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah.

573
00:56:07,985 --> 00:56:12,589
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a reason on its own, all of that, but you want to see its there-ness, so to speak.

574
00:56:13,430 --> 00:56:14,951
[SPEAKER_04]: That it's impenetrable to you.

575
00:56:15,351 --> 00:56:19,395
[SPEAKER_04]: All you can do is resist it, or lean towards it, or ignore it.

576
00:56:19,935 --> 00:56:21,036
[SPEAKER_04]: Nothing else you can do about it.

577
00:56:22,317 --> 00:56:24,459
[SPEAKER_04]: It's impenetrable to any of your efforts.

578
00:56:24,499 --> 00:56:25,320
[SPEAKER_04]: Everything is around it.

579
00:56:27,802 --> 00:56:28,983
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the nature of any feeling.

580
00:56:30,104 --> 00:56:31,365
[SPEAKER_04]: Pleasant, unpleasant or neutral.

581
00:56:35,217 --> 00:56:36,978
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the nature of any aggregate, for that matter.

582
00:56:37,118 --> 00:56:39,419
[SPEAKER_04]: It's unownable.

583
00:56:42,801 --> 00:56:48,344
[SPEAKER_02]: You said all you can do is lean towards it, lean away from it, or ignore it.

584
00:56:48,764 --> 00:56:52,486
[SPEAKER_02]: But there must be an alternative, otherwise there's no freedom from suffering.

585
00:56:53,967 --> 00:56:54,187
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

586
00:56:54,928 --> 00:56:59,330
[SPEAKER_04]: Alternative is to not lean towards it, not lean away from it, and not ignore it.

587
00:57:00,551 --> 00:57:04,233
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so there is... yeah, obviously, there's other freedom from suffering.

588
00:57:05,510 --> 00:57:06,511
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, no, that's one option.

589
00:57:07,451 --> 00:57:12,274
[SPEAKER_02]: That's really... Four options, let's say.

590
00:57:13,035 --> 00:57:15,256
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you don't have four feelings at the same time.

591
00:57:15,836 --> 00:57:16,857
[SPEAKER_04]: You only have one feeling.

592
00:57:17,829 --> 00:57:21,372
[SPEAKER_04]: Which means you're either leaning towards, leaning away, or ignoring it.

593
00:57:21,412 --> 00:57:25,415
[SPEAKER_04]: Which means you only have one option to stop doing what you're doing at a time towards that feeling.

594
00:57:26,296 --> 00:57:28,318
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when they asked the Buddha, how did you cross the shore?

595
00:57:28,418 --> 00:57:32,542
[SPEAKER_04]: Because he said, if I kind of went against the waves, I got tired.

596
00:57:32,702 --> 00:57:34,683
[SPEAKER_04]: If I pulled backwards, I got swept away.

597
00:57:35,164 --> 00:57:37,926
[SPEAKER_04]: If I was trying to kind of stay in the middle, I sank.

598
00:57:39,067 --> 00:57:41,269
[SPEAKER_04]: Also, Master Gautama, how did you cross the shore?

599
00:57:41,649 --> 00:57:45,553
[SPEAKER_04]: He said, by not fighting the waves, by not pulling backwards, and by not standing still.

600
00:57:47,630 --> 00:57:51,031
[SPEAKER_04]: Which means, at every given time, there is one feeling enduring that you...

601
00:57:51,111 --> 00:57:54,032
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, if you try to understand it theoretically, it's gonna be a mind-boggle.

602
00:57:54,832 --> 00:57:56,953
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, the three, and the three, and...

603
00:57:56,973 --> 00:58:02,175
[SPEAKER_04]: I have to find this kind of, basically, self-contorted position where I engage in all these three aspects.

604
00:58:02,195 --> 00:58:02,855
[SPEAKER_04]: That's impossible.

605
00:58:05,916 --> 00:58:09,297
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it's recognition that there is only one feeling at a time being felt.

606
00:58:10,797 --> 00:58:15,999
[SPEAKER_04]: Which means, by default, you will either be leaning towards it, away from it, or ignoring it.

607
00:58:16,720 --> 00:58:18,941
[SPEAKER_04]: Which means, that's... you only have one option.

608
00:58:19,522 --> 00:58:22,143
[SPEAKER_04]: Do not lean that... do not do that which you're doing at a time.

609
00:58:22,404 --> 00:58:23,344
[SPEAKER_04]: Whichever leaning that is.

610
00:58:25,926 --> 00:58:37,053
[SPEAKER_04]: And you cultivate that every single time, when there is a present enduring feeling, for the extent necessary, for seven days, seven weeks, seven months, seven years, the wisdom will have to arise.

611
00:58:38,233 --> 00:58:43,877
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you have basically been preventing craving from... from... from growing further.

612
00:58:45,742 --> 00:58:54,084
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, as I said, craving, bhāva, whatever you want to take it, whichever aspect you approach it with, needs maintenance, needs sustaining.

613
00:58:55,285 --> 00:58:57,085
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a static thing, in that sense.

614
00:59:06,268 --> 00:59:07,528
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how do I do all these things?

615
00:59:08,368 --> 00:59:14,770
[SPEAKER_04]: Discern as clearly as you can, peripherally, what is the presently enduring feeling, mood you are under.

616
00:59:17,622 --> 00:59:22,066
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, don't try to fix it, get rid of it, explain it, find your craving in it.

617
00:59:23,567 --> 00:59:24,989
[SPEAKER_04]: Just try to not forget about it.

618
00:59:25,129 --> 00:59:31,655
[SPEAKER_04]: Let it endure, while you don't specifically try to think about it either, like, oh, I'm feeling like this, I'm feeling like that.

619
00:59:31,675 --> 00:59:34,517
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you are feeling like that, whether you say to yourself how you feel or not.

620
00:59:38,120 --> 00:59:41,944
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the clearer it becomes on that peripheral level, the less you would be resisting it.

621
00:59:42,883 --> 00:59:45,685
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, again, you are not doing the craving.

622
00:59:45,725 --> 00:59:49,447
[SPEAKER_04]: Craving is the result of you not knowing the nature of feeling, for example.

623
00:59:50,947 --> 00:59:53,949
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can't just stop craving as a matter of choice.

624
00:59:55,050 --> 01:00:06,656
[SPEAKER_04]: But you can remove the necessary basis for craving, which is lack of knowledge, ignoring, lack of understanding, lack of clarity in regard to that which endures.

625
01:00:06,756 --> 01:00:07,176
One way to

626
01:00:12,212 --> 01:00:24,854
[SPEAKER_02]: To develop clarity in regard to that which endures, is it to discern the signs of the mind?

627
01:00:24,874 --> 01:00:39,537
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for example, yeah, an angry or lustful mind will have... Yeah, you can discern the presently enduring mood as a phenomenon.

628
01:00:40,734 --> 01:00:48,845
[SPEAKER_04]: Not as an object of your attention, but as a thing that endures there, kind of, regardless of whether you attend to it or not.

629
01:00:48,866 --> 01:00:55,335
[SPEAKER_04]: That's already kind of discerning the shapes of your mind, the signs of it, the characteristics of it enduring on that general level.

630
01:00:56,759 --> 01:01:01,962
[SPEAKER_04]: Because the problem people have is, even when they get the idea, they're used to engaging ideas only on the particular level.

631
01:01:02,203 --> 01:01:04,244
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I have to make it an object of my attention.

632
01:01:04,464 --> 01:01:06,005
[SPEAKER_04]: Ah, so the... And then it ceases.

633
01:01:06,025 --> 01:01:10,768
[SPEAKER_04]: You cease to be on the level you must be, which is the level of present enduring phenomena, which is always the background.

634
01:01:20,394 --> 01:01:21,915
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you cease to be mindful, basically.

635
01:01:23,596 --> 01:01:24,237
[SPEAKER_03]: Mindful, properly.

636
01:01:26,682 --> 01:01:30,564
[SPEAKER_04]: So, sensory strain needs to be guiding, or rather guarding, your behavior.

637
01:01:32,025 --> 01:01:37,367
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, this is something within that sensory strain, within unbroken sīla, seeing the danger in the slightest fault.

638
01:01:38,147 --> 01:01:49,413
[SPEAKER_04]: Within that, now, you start seeing the present enduring mood, and you never cross the threshold of acting out of sensuality and anger and giving in to the cause aspect, which will kind of undo your work.

639
01:01:50,273 --> 01:01:54,255
[SPEAKER_04]: So, even if sometimes you're not clear about it, you still have your sīla around as your guard.

640
01:01:55,875 --> 01:01:57,215
[SPEAKER_04]: And you don't do these things.

641
01:01:58,536 --> 01:02:07,078
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, when the mind comes down, oh, you get to re-discern that mood back on, when it's not as heavy, whatever else... whatever pressure was exhibiting over you.

642
01:02:08,038 --> 01:02:11,659
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's how you train your mind within that right context of sense restraint.

643
01:02:13,100 --> 01:02:21,902
[SPEAKER_04]: And the more you do it, the clearer you become about it, which means less and less active restraint you need to do, because there is... the passion is diminishing.

644
01:02:23,314 --> 01:02:25,759
[SPEAKER_04]: Craving is diminishing because ignoring is diminishing.

645
01:02:27,041 --> 01:02:29,365
[SPEAKER_04]: Until things become apparent to the sufficient extent.

646
01:02:30,668 --> 01:02:31,609
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's a constant work.

647
01:02:32,431 --> 01:02:34,555
[SPEAKER_04]: There's absolutely nothing mystical about it.

648
01:02:36,618 --> 01:02:37,899
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just constant drilling.

649
01:02:38,220 --> 01:02:40,602
[SPEAKER_04]: I remember, I mean, you probably heard me saying that in other talks.

650
01:02:40,642 --> 01:02:44,145
[SPEAKER_04]: When I started reading the suttas, you know, you come as a layman, just new to it.

651
01:02:44,786 --> 01:02:47,669
[SPEAKER_04]: You have some ideas, but a lot of it is unclear.

652
01:02:48,369 --> 01:02:54,855
[SPEAKER_04]: And you have these ideas of enlightenment, and you know, if you do these things rightly, enlightenment happens to you, and then you're fixed, and then you're always in the bliss.

653
01:02:55,716 --> 01:02:58,517
[SPEAKER_04]: But I remember, I was puzzled by reading the suttas and the Buddha.

654
01:02:58,757 --> 01:02:59,958
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, he trains himself that.

655
01:02:59,978 --> 01:03:01,239
[SPEAKER_04]: And he keeps talking about the training.

656
01:03:01,879 --> 01:03:05,481
[SPEAKER_04]: And to me, that was completely the opposite of why I expected enlightenment to be.

657
01:03:05,501 --> 01:03:07,161
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, wait, how do you train that?

658
01:03:07,622 --> 01:03:13,264
[SPEAKER_04]: If it's the real thing, it should just happen to you as like a, you know, a divine providence just sort of opens up for you.

659
01:03:14,245 --> 01:03:15,105
[SPEAKER_04]: Not like training.

660
01:03:15,185 --> 01:03:16,666
[SPEAKER_04]: Training, it's like you making it.

661
01:03:17,166 --> 01:03:18,887
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, oh, yeah, it's exactly that.

662
01:03:20,223 --> 01:03:24,707
[SPEAKER_04]: You making your mind immovable in regard to things that move you, because the mind is weaker than them.

663
01:03:25,207 --> 01:03:27,149
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's what the Buddha said in the suttas.

664
01:03:27,909 --> 01:03:29,811
[SPEAKER_04]: Arahant is not moved by his mind.

665
01:03:29,911 --> 01:03:31,712
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the mind that gets moved by the Arahant.

666
01:03:32,293 --> 01:03:32,973
[SPEAKER_04]: Like his thoughts.

667
01:03:33,013 --> 01:03:35,976
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm not remember exactly, but it was the point is, he's not the one that moves.

668
01:03:37,017 --> 01:03:38,017
[SPEAKER_04]: The mind moves, not him.

669
01:03:38,758 --> 01:03:42,601
[SPEAKER_04]: Because he developed himself more than his thoughts that used to move him around.

670
01:03:45,483 --> 01:03:47,385
[SPEAKER_04]: So he doesn't get overpowered by his thoughts.

671
01:03:47,545 --> 01:03:48,646
[SPEAKER_04]: He overpowers the thoughts.

672
01:03:49,367 --> 01:03:49,827
[SPEAKER_04]: Naturally.

673
01:03:51,118 --> 01:03:57,443
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's all those similes, like a mountain and earth, water, fire, immovable, regardless of what comes there to move you.

674
01:03:59,344 --> 01:04:01,346
[SPEAKER_04]: So, as I said, there's nothing mystical about it.

675
01:04:01,486 --> 01:04:05,689
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just a constant work and drilling on the basis of precepts, seeing the danger.

676
01:04:05,749 --> 01:04:08,491
[SPEAKER_04]: And you read the suttas, and that's what I said countless number of times.

677
01:04:10,433 --> 01:04:14,016
[SPEAKER_04]: Ah, the man, out of faith, young man, leaves his home, goes into homelessness.

678
01:04:15,522 --> 01:04:19,066
[SPEAKER_04]: Whether he walks, sits, eats, talks, he doesn't tolerate the reasoned sensual desire, the reasoned thought of ill will.

679
01:04:35,718 --> 01:04:38,660
[SPEAKER_04]: And even then, I remember reading it the first time, you think, okay, this is all...

680
01:04:38,800 --> 01:04:42,342
[SPEAKER_04]: I'll do this, but then, what do I do for the actual enlightenment?

681
01:04:42,362 --> 01:04:45,244
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you want something specific that will result in this great experience.

682
01:04:45,284 --> 01:04:45,424
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

683
01:04:45,744 --> 01:04:46,585
[SPEAKER_04]: This is what you do.

684
01:04:47,625 --> 01:04:49,106
[SPEAKER_04]: But you just need to do it thoroughly.

685
01:04:50,247 --> 01:04:54,950
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you realize, oh, I don't suffer like the simile of the carpenter using the axe handle.

686
01:04:56,130 --> 01:05:03,515
[SPEAKER_04]: He doesn't know how much his axe handle has worn off when he uses it, but it is wearing thinner each time he uses it.

687
01:05:04,247 --> 01:05:10,174
[SPEAKER_04]: Until one day, he comes into his workshop, picks up the axe and says, oh, my entire handle has worn thin.

688
01:05:10,454 --> 01:05:11,135
[SPEAKER_04]: I have to replace it.

689
01:05:11,996 --> 01:05:16,901
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's how the monk who purifies his mind one day, he just recognizes, oh, look, nothing for me to do anymore.

690
01:05:17,562 --> 01:05:18,443
[SPEAKER_04]: I am truly free.

691
01:05:20,848 --> 01:05:22,950
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why, again, it cannot be taken away.

692
01:05:23,731 --> 01:05:25,092
[SPEAKER_04]: It did not come through your senses.

693
01:05:25,573 --> 01:05:29,436
[SPEAKER_04]: It did not come on the basis of any being, anything happening to you out of your control.

694
01:05:29,917 --> 01:05:30,037
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

695
01:05:30,157 --> 01:05:36,923
[SPEAKER_04]: It came on account of you understanding the absolute nature, in that most universal sense, of your present, enduring experience.

696
01:05:38,465 --> 01:05:40,407
[SPEAKER_04]: Not once, not twice, but thousands of times.

697
01:05:42,509 --> 01:05:43,930
[SPEAKER_04]: You trained yourself sufficiently.

698
01:06:02,270 --> 01:06:23,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you said, regarding feeling, that the extent to which I either lean towards, or lean away, or am ignorant about it, is tied to not understanding it.

699
01:06:24,399 --> 01:06:26,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Not understanding that presently enduring feeling.

700
01:06:27,260 --> 01:06:30,442
[SPEAKER_01]: And I often read in the suttas that

701
01:06:33,492 --> 01:06:52,268
[SPEAKER_01]: This understanding takes place by directly discerning this presently enduring phenomenon, like feeling that is now there, that is now enduring, and then also discerning what it is dependent upon, what it is also there.

702
01:06:52,669 --> 01:06:53,850
[SPEAKER_04]: That would become apparent.

703
01:06:54,842 --> 01:07:01,327
[SPEAKER_04]: See, already, like, exactly, discerning the present enduring phenomenon can only be done on the present enduring phenomenon.

704
01:07:01,868 --> 01:07:10,135
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's that important point, distinction I was making, where when you end up thinking about the idea that you assume feeling to be,

705
01:07:11,565 --> 01:07:16,727
[SPEAKER_04]: And you assume it to stand for the feeling, which means you're not discerning it on the level of the phenomenon.

706
01:07:17,067 --> 01:07:21,409
[SPEAKER_04]: You're now just thinking about your idea, which can only have relative results, not the result of the wisdom.

707
01:07:22,189 --> 01:07:27,951
[SPEAKER_04]: So it's... that's like... that's when the experience as a whole, where things converge, if you go back to that, that's where you would discern it.

708
01:07:28,291 --> 01:07:29,552
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's when it would become apparent.

709
01:07:30,692 --> 01:07:33,033
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, oh, it's dependent upon this.

710
01:07:33,213 --> 01:07:35,794
[SPEAKER_04]: You cannot have feeling without having this.

711
01:07:35,814 --> 01:07:37,194
[SPEAKER_04]: Either way, I'm redundant.

712
01:07:37,334 --> 01:07:39,835
[SPEAKER_04]: My sense of ownership is... there's no room for it.

713
01:07:40,615 --> 01:07:43,977
[SPEAKER_04]: Whether I ignore it or not, these things don't know about me, so to speak.

714
01:07:44,057 --> 01:07:46,077
[SPEAKER_04]: So, me...

715
01:07:46,277 --> 01:07:48,138
[SPEAKER_04]: I can only be affected by the feeling.

716
01:07:48,398 --> 01:07:49,779
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a two-way relationship.

717
01:07:49,899 --> 01:07:51,739
[SPEAKER_04]: It's only me who suffers on account of feeling.

718
01:07:52,119 --> 01:07:53,520
[SPEAKER_04]: Feeling is unaware that I exist.

719
01:07:54,601 --> 01:08:02,865
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the more these characteristics of the present enduring feeling are kind of become apparent, the more dispassion towards it you will experience.

720
01:08:03,005 --> 01:08:09,108
[SPEAKER_04]: The less ability to crave and lean to... Because you always lean, because you still assume you can make a difference to it.

721
01:08:12,650 --> 01:08:15,532
[SPEAKER_04]: And you assume you can make a difference, because you're not clear about it.

722
01:08:15,552 --> 01:08:15,852
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry?

723
01:08:17,313 --> 01:08:21,295
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's actually part of one single insight.

724
01:08:22,113 --> 01:08:24,054
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah.

725
01:08:24,154 --> 01:08:30,497
[SPEAKER_04]: It's basically just taking the same insight further and further, and more and more things become apparent through the same insight.

726
01:08:30,517 --> 01:08:30,757
[SPEAKER_04]: Same.

727
01:08:31,098 --> 01:08:33,379
[SPEAKER_04]: The Four Noble Truths is just one Noble Truth.

728
01:08:34,499 --> 01:08:35,500
[SPEAKER_04]: It's the truth of suffering.

729
01:08:37,121 --> 01:08:39,082
[SPEAKER_04]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

730
01:08:39,122 --> 01:08:39,722
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the truth.

731
01:08:40,909 --> 01:08:46,854
[SPEAKER_04]: Through that truth, you know the absence of craving means absence of suffering, regardless of how disagreeable the circumstances are.

732
01:08:47,455 --> 01:08:50,938
[SPEAKER_04]: Through that truth, you know then how to sustain the absent craving.

733
01:08:51,458 --> 01:08:52,219
[SPEAKER_04]: You know the path.

734
01:08:52,379 --> 01:08:53,600
[SPEAKER_04]: You know the way leading out.

735
01:08:54,561 --> 01:08:55,982
[SPEAKER_04]: But it's not one, two, three, four.

736
01:08:59,485 --> 01:09:05,050
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just one, different aspects of one, then you get through the other one through the same framework.

737
01:09:09,077 --> 01:09:16,123
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why somebody said that the one who sees one of the Four Noble Truths, sees them all, actually.

738
01:09:17,004 --> 01:09:17,184
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

739
01:09:19,346 --> 01:09:26,553
[SPEAKER_04]: The one who understands, basically, the principle of craving-suffering-suffering-craving, understands the arahantship.

740
01:09:35,601 --> 01:10:02,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I discern a presently enduring feeling, I really discern it and I also see that I'm pressured by it and that I can basically do nothing about this feeling being there and being pressured about it and that my leaning away from it and my craving towards it and my aversion towards it or my ignorance is

741
01:10:04,010 --> 01:10:06,091
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

742
01:10:06,852 --> 01:10:23,800
[SPEAKER_04]: You kind of... you end up sort of in a... in a... in a... like a little... little irony of... oh, you're pressured by the feeling, which is why you give in to the thoughts of trying to understand the feeling that is rooted in craving that maintains the pressure.

743
01:10:23,820 --> 01:10:25,601
[SPEAKER_04]: But you do everything to be free from the pressure.

744
01:10:28,362 --> 01:10:30,143
[SPEAKER_04]: But it has to be like that in the beginning.

745
01:10:30,163 --> 01:10:30,764
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what I meant.

746
01:10:31,024 --> 01:10:33,045
[SPEAKER_04]: Craving is to be abandoned by the means of craving.

747
01:10:33,904 --> 01:10:37,948
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, first you crave through your thoughts to be free from craving and pressure of the feeling.

748
01:10:38,408 --> 01:10:46,676
[SPEAKER_04]: But, see, through developing more and more correct mindfulness, you get to see, ah, so I can think these things without craving, and when there is craving, I will not think it.

749
01:10:47,197 --> 01:10:52,802
[SPEAKER_04]: So, because of the previous cravings in the past of wanting to be free from suffering, now you actually ended up practicing dhamma correctly.

750
01:10:53,863 --> 01:10:56,246
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you abandon craving by the means of craving.

751
01:10:58,407 --> 01:11:02,728
[SPEAKER_04]: But not, you abandon craving through constantly engaging repetitively through the same craving.

752
01:11:02,768 --> 01:11:04,308
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you perpetuate it.

753
01:11:04,328 --> 01:11:10,529
[SPEAKER_04]: You abandon craving through learning how to not engage with craving anymore, for which you had to crave initially, to that idea.

754
01:11:11,049 --> 01:11:16,971
[SPEAKER_04]: I want... ideal to develop, has to be affected with craving initially, and that's fine.

755
01:11:17,471 --> 01:11:20,531
[SPEAKER_04]: But you want to see through it.

756
01:11:22,312 --> 01:11:24,152
[SPEAKER_04]: Same with the conceit, in that same sutta.

757
01:11:25,764 --> 01:11:27,686
[SPEAKER_04]: Conceit is to be abandoned by the means of conceit.

758
01:11:27,906 --> 01:11:30,968
[SPEAKER_04]: Such and such monk hears that another monk became an arahant.

759
01:11:31,009 --> 01:11:33,971
[SPEAKER_04]: He thinks to himself, if he can do it, I can do it.

760
01:11:35,452 --> 01:11:36,613
[SPEAKER_04]: Really conceited thought.

761
01:11:37,334 --> 01:11:38,115
[SPEAKER_04]: Quite unwholesome.

762
01:11:38,795 --> 01:11:41,177
[SPEAKER_04]: But because of it, he actually starts restraining more.

763
01:11:42,098 --> 01:11:43,239
[SPEAKER_04]: Starts reading the suttas more.

764
01:11:44,160 --> 01:11:47,823
[SPEAKER_04]: Starts really paying that attention to what's happening in the background of his mind.

765
01:11:48,964 --> 01:11:50,505
[SPEAKER_04]: And he approves the conceit eventually.

766
01:11:51,166 --> 01:11:51,646
[SPEAKER_04]: And there you go.

767
01:11:52,006 --> 01:11:52,567
[SPEAKER_04]: He's free now.

